The time a datagram ARRIVED, handed to the acknowledgement machinery where quic-go hands it.

WHAT THE REFERENCE DOES. quic-go reads the socket in a goroutine of its own that does nothing else
(Transport.listen, vendor/quic-go/transport.go:547-549) and stamps each datagram the moment the
read returns — `rcvTime: monotime.Now()` (sys_conn_oob.go:169 on the OOB path, sys_conn.go:107 on
the plain one hysteria takes under Salamander). That stamp, not the time the connection gets round
to the packet, is what every acknowledgement is processed with:

    handleErr = c.handleAckFrame(ackFrame, encLevel, rcvTime)  (vendor/quic-go/connection.go:1864)
    func (h *sentPacketHandler) ReceivedAck(..., rcvTime)
                                  (vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:403)
        h.detectLostPackets(rcvTime, encLevel)                         (:462)
        cc.OnPacketAcked(p.PacketNumber, p.Length, priorInFlight, rcvTime)   (:470)
        cex.OnCongestionEventEx(priorInFlight, rcvTime, ...)          (:487)

So hysteria's BBR — ported here as patch 0009 — measures delivery with arrival times.

WHAT NGTCP2 DOES. It already tells the two apart inside: conn_recv_pkt, conn_recv_ack and
ngtcp2_rtb_recv_ack all take a `pkt_ts` beside `ts`, and the comments on them say that for a
buffered packet pkt_ts is earlier. But the public ngtcp2_conn_read_pkt has one timestamp and passes
it as both (conn_recv_cpkt: `conn_recv_pkt(..., ts, ts)`), and inside ngtcp2_rtb_recv_ack only the
RTT sample reads pkt_ts — the controller (on_pkt_acked, on_ack_recv), the rate sampler and loss
detection all read `ts`.

WHAT IT COST, measured 2026-09-21 with kernel receive timestamps (SO_TIMESTAMPNS) on the one-way
stand: an acknowledgement sat 118 us on average between arriving and being processed, a third of
them over 50 us and 86 of 4 591 over a millisecond — the time this client's single loop spends
packing before it reads again. The controller saw every one of those as a later ack: longer
rounds, compressed delivery intervals, a lower bandwidth estimate (457 against the reference's 660
Mbit/s in PROBE_BW on the same stand).

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES.
  - ngtcp2_conn_read_pkt_rx: ngtcp2_conn_read_pkt plus the arrival time, 0 meaning unknown.
  - conn_recv_cpkt hands that time down as pkt_ts, never later than ts and never earlier than the
    last one it handed down: arrival times are monotonic on the wire, and the floor keeps them so
    across the handshake, whose packets are still read with ts.
  - ngtcp2_rtb_recv_ack gives pkt_ts to on_pkt_acked, on_ack_recv, on_spurious_congestion and loss
    detection — the four places quic-go passes rcvTime. The RTT sample already used it. Timers,
    logging and everything that must stay monotonic keep ts.

WHAT IT BOUGHT, measured the day it went in, and it is honest to say little. One-way stand,
reference on plain Go: back to back 93 and 91 % against 90-92 before and the reference's 97;
holes of 1-5 ms in the acknowledgements from the node 451 and 716 against 548-1073 before and
the reference's 248-290. Xiaomi 4A against the live node, one binary at a time, 4 x 5 MB up:
2.75 · 2.75 MB/s before, 2.77 · 2.84 · 2.79 after, CPU per 20 MB 917 · 904 against
890 · 849 · 896. All inside the spread. It is here because it is what the reference does.

Applies on top of 0010.
